olve on GPU up to some N. Or CUSPARSE. Let me search.\n\nLet me also think about the sequential baseline: the fastest sequential implementation. For a tridiagonal solve, Thomas algorithm is O(N) with 8N operations, memory-bandwidth bound. On GPU, could use a single-thread Thomas (very slow due to latency) or use CUB scan for bidiagonal. For tridiagonal, the \"sequential\" version on GPU would be... Actually a hybrid: the fastest sequential implementation we time is the Thomas algorithm. On a GPU, a pure sequential Thomas is memory-latency bound. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scope. The claim is specifically about the block-bidiagonal Newton correction (which the framer says is block lower-bidiagonal, and the option says \"tridiagonal/bidiagonal\"). A bidiagonal solve = linear recurrence = scan. So the natural build:\n\n1. Baseline sequential: forward substitution / Thomas for bidiagonal (O(T) sequential).\n2. Parallel: cyclic reduction (PCR) for tridiagonal, or scan-based (Blelloch) for bidiagonal in O(log T).\n\nActually the cleanest and most defensible: implement both a tridiagonal solver (Thomas sequential vs PCR parallel) and note bidiagonal is a special case. The option text says \"block-bidiagonal Newton correction (a tridiagonal/bidiagonal system)\". Let me build a block-tridiagonal / block-bidiagonal solver.\n\nTo keep it concrete and cheap, I'll build:\n- A correctness reference: an exact solve via cuSPARSE/cuSOLVER or numpy on CPU.\n- Sequential baseline: hand-tuned Thomas algorithm (O(T)), single GPU, or CUB-based scan for bidiagonal.\n- Parallel: parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) kernel in CUDA (O(log T) rounds).\n- Also compare against cuSPARSE's gtsv (which internally uses... actually cusparse<t>gtsv uses a hybrid). Hmm, but to measure PCR specifically we implement it ourselves.\n\nSweep T = system size. Measure wall-clock. Check residual ||A x - b|| vs reference.\n\nCost: A100 40GB at $1.99/hr (1 GPU) is plenty. Sweeps of tridiagonal solves up