ocessors\" — A100 has 6912 CUDA cores / 108 SMs. This matters for the crossover: PCR needs ~T processors, so for T ≫ 6912, the O(log T) still holds but with waves. Actually each SM can handle many threads (2048 threads/SM → ~221k threads). So \"processors\" ~ 100k threads. The crossover: PCR wins when log2(T) rounds × per-round cost < T steps of sequential. On GPU, sequential Thomas is memory-bound (latency ~ hundreds of ns per dependent step), so even at T=1e5, sequential is very slow; PCR should win at small T.\n\n5. Cost numbers: A100 40GB 1-GPU $1.99/hr (from price sheet, CITED). A100 80GB SXM 8-GPU $2.79/hr. We probably only need 1 GPU. The tridiagonal solve benchmark fits in a few GB.\n\n6. Memory: system of T block-tridiagonal with block size d. For T=1e7, d=1 (scalar), 3 arrays of 1e7 doubles = 240 MB. Fits easily. For d=16 blocks, T=1e6 → 16*16*3*1e6*8 = ~6 GB. Fine on A100 40GB.\n\nLet me now think about what \"build\" concretely:\n- Write CUDA kernels: `thomas` (sequential, one thread per... actually Thomas sequential on GPU = one thread marching; but that's latency-bound. Better sequential baseline: use a single thread or use CUB's `InclusiveScan`? No — for tridiagonal we need Thomas). \n\nHmm, actually the honest \"fastest sequential implementation\" for a bidiagonal (the actual Newton correction) is a scan (prefix sum), which CUB does in O(log N) parallel already. For tridiagonal, sequential = Thomas.\n\nLet me simplify and be precise about the two cases:\n\nThe option text: \"the block-bidiagonal Newton correction (a tridiagonal/bidiagonal system)\". The Jacobian of multiple shooting is block lower-bidiagonal. So the solve is a block lower-bidiagonal solve = block forward substitution = block linear recurrence.\n\nBlock lower-bidiagonal: L has blocks A_t on diagonal and B_t on subdiagonal. Solve L x = b: x_0 = A_0^{-1} b_0; x_t = A_t^{-1}(b_t - B_t x_{t-1}). This is a forward substitution, O(T) deep. Parallel cyclic reduction for bidiagonal = prefix sum / scan of the recurrence. Actually PCR for tridiagonal generalizes; bidiagonal is a special case.\n\nTo keep it concrete and match the option's \"tridiagonal/bidiagonal\", I'll build a general block-tridiagonal solver (Thomas sequential vs PCR parallel), which subsumes bidiagonal. Or build a scalar tridiagonal first (simpler, matches the classic PCR literature), then block generalization.\n\nActually let me keep it focused: The claim to test is \"PCR solves tridiagonal in O(log N) with N-fold parallelism, dropping per-iteration depth O(T)→O(log T).\" So build:\n1. Thomas (sequential, O(N)) — baseline_sequential.\n2. PCR (parallel, O(log N) rounds) — the parallel method.\n3. Reference: numpy/cuSPARSE exact solve — anchor.\n\nBenchmark wall-clock vs N. Verify correctness. Determine crossover.\n\nNow compute_axis: quantity = \"number of equations N (time steps)\" — this is the chain length. levels = e.g. [2^10, 2^13, 2^16, 2^20, 2^24] = [1024, 8192, 65536, 1048576, 16777216].\n\npredictions:\n- if_lever: \"PCR wall-clock grows as log N; beyond crossover N it beats Thomas, so width shortens the sequential part.\"\n- if_not: \"PCR never beats Thomas at feasible N because GPU has too few threads and extra work dominates; width does not shorten it.\"\n\ndecision_rule: compare measured PCR wall-clock vs Thomas wall-clock at the largest N (2^24) on the A100: if PCR < Thomas there, if_lever; threshold number.\n\nLet me now search for sources to cite. I need:\n1. PCR notes PDF (already in option). Let me fetch it to get the exact sentence.\n2. Thomas algorithm O(N) — Wikipedia.\n3. GPU tridiagonal solvers paper (Zhang, Cohen, Owens 2010) for the crossover / hybrid finding.\n4. A100 SM count / CUDA cores — NVIDIA spec page.\n5. cuSPARSE tridiagonal solver existence — NVIDIA docs (for the independent code / anchor).\n6. Maybe cuSOLVER docs.\n\nLet me do web searches and fetches. I should fetch the PCR notes PDF to get exact text. Also fetch the Zhang et al. paper (open access