   {
    "role": "tool",
    "tool_call_id": "call_01_8Chla745fgFoWqMVTLkp7195",
    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_d86039bcced6c095f36ab6edb0c21957\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/sites/default/files/pubs/2010-01_Fast-Tridiagonal-Solvers/Zhang_Fast_2009.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - Research at NVIDIA\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2009-11-02\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nYao Zhang\\nUniversity of California, Davis yaozhang@ucdavis.edu\\nJonathan Cohen\\nNVIDIA jocohen@nvidia.com\\nJohn D. Owens\\nUniversity of California, Davis jowens@ece.ucdavis.edu\\nAbstract\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recur- sive doubling (RD).\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD al- gorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\\n1. Introduction\\nIn this work, we study the performance of tridiagonal linear sys- tem solvers on a GPU. We use this study to develop an approach to measure, analyze, and optimize the performance of GPU programs.\\nTo copy otherwise, to republish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires prior specific permission and/or a fee. PPoPP’10, January 9–14, 2010, Bangalore, India. Copyright c ⃝ 2010 ACM 978-1-60558-708-0/10/01...$10.00\\nwith the basic algorithms and just tried to remove their bottlenecks.\\nFast solutions to a tridiagonal system of linear equations are crit- ical for many scientific and engineering problems, as well as real- time or interactive applications in computer graphics, video games and animation films [19, 20, 25].\\nWhile all previous GPU tridiagonal solvers that we are aware of use cyclic reduction, we identify four other techniques that have superior performance on a current genera- tion GPU, including parallel cyclic reduction, recursive doubling, and their hybrid variants.\\nOther hybrid ap- proaches have been studied in a CPU cluster environment. Johnsson and Ho studied hybrid solvers using sub-structuring method, Gaus- sian elimination and cyclic reduction with the goal of minimizing overall computation and communication costs [15].\\nSun and Zhang proposed a two-level hybrid method with a first-level solver based on the Sherman-Morrison modification formula and an application- dependent second-level solver [29].\\n4. GPU Implementation\\nWe implement the five tridiagonal solvers on an NVIDIA GTX 280 GPU using the CUDA programming model [23].\\nParallel multigrid for anisotropic elliptic equations. J. Parallel Distrib. Comput. , 61(1):96–114, 2001.\\n[25] S. Sengupta, M. Harris, Y. Zhang, and J. D. Owens. Scan primitives for GPU computing. In Graphics Hardware 2007 , pages 97–106, Aug. 2007.\\n[26] S. Sengupta, A. E. Lefohn, and J. D. Owens.\\nZhang, and L. M. Ni. Efficient tridiagonal solvers on multicomputers. IEEE Transactions on Computers , C-41(3):286–296, Mar. 1992.\\n[29] X.-H. Sun and W. Zhang. A parallel two-level hybrid method for tridiagonal systems and its application to fast Poisson solvers. IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Sys- tems , PDS-15(2):97–106, Feb. 2004.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2010-01_fast-tridiagonal-solvers-gpu\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU | Research\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1. Publications\\n2. Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nFast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nWe develop an approach to measure, analyze, and optimize the performance of GPU programs in terms of memory access, computation, and control overhead.We find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD algorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively. Our GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\\nAuthors\\nYao Zhang (UC Davis)\\nJonathan Cohen (NVIDIA)\\nJohn Owens (UC Davis)\\nPublication Date\\nFriday, January 1, 2010\\nPublished in\\n15th ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on Principles and Practice of Parallel Programming (…\\nResearch Area\\nAlgorithms and Numerical Methods\\nUploaded Files\\nZhang_Fast_2009.pdf1.32 MB\\nCopyright\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7b441610\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU - eScholarship\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Institute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nFast Tridiagonal Solvers on the GPU\\n2010\\n* Zhang, Yao ;\\n* Cohen, Jonathan ;\\n* Owens, John D.\\nPublished Web Location\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/1693453.1693472\\n... Main Content Metrics Author & Article Info\\nAbstract\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nWe develop an approach to measure, analyze, and optimize the performance of GPU programs in terms of memory access, computation, and control overhead.\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nWe find that CR enjoys linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps and bank conflicts, while PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step.\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nTo combine the benefits of the basic algorithms, we propose hybrid CR+PCR and CR+RD algorithms, which improve the performance of PCR, RD and CR by 21%, 31% and 61% respectively.\\nInstitute for Data Analysis and Visualization UC Davis\\nOur GPU solvers achieve up to a 28x speedup over a sequential LAPACK solver, and a 12x speedup over a multi-threaded CPU solver.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/ipdps/2011/4385a956/12OmNrJiCFY\",\n      \"title\": \"An Auto-tuned Method for Solving Large Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2011-05-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Andrew Davidson Yao Zhang [John D. Owens](/csdl/search/default?type=author&givenName=John D.&surname=Owens)\\nDownload PDF SHARE ARTICLE\\nGenerate Citation\\nAbstract\\nWe present a multi-stage method for solving large tridiagonal systems on the GPU. Previously large tridiagonal systems cannot be efficiently solved due to the limitation of on-chip shared memory size. We tackle this problem by splitting the systems into smaller ones and then solving them on-chip.\\nThe multi-stage characteristic of our method, together with various workloads and GPUs of different capabilities, obligates an auto-tuning strategy to carefully select the switch points between computation stages.\\nIn particular, we show two ways to effectively prune the tuning space and thus avoid an impractical exhaustive search: (1) apply algorithmic knowledge to decouple tuning parameters, and (2) estimate search starting points based on GPU architecture parameters.\\nWe demonstrate that auto-tuning is a powerful tool that improves the performance by up to 5x, saves 17% and 32% of execution time on average respectively over static and dynamic tuning, and enables our multi-stage solver to outperform the Intel MKL tridiagonal solver on many parallel tridiagonal systems by 6-11x.\\nLike what you’re reading?\\n* Solving tridiagonal systems on a GPU 2013 20th International Conference on High Performance Computing (HiPC)\\n* A Scalable Tridiagonal Solver for GPUs 2011 International Conference on Parallel Processing\\n* Solving Large Problem Sizes of Index-Digit Algorithms on GPU: FFT and Tridiagonal System Solvers IEEE Transactions on Computers\\n* Cyclic Reduction Tridiagonal Solvers on GPUs Applied to Mixed-Precision Multigrid IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\nCopyright 2026 IEEE - All rights reserved. A public charity, IEEE is the world’s largest technical professional organization dedicated to advancing technology for the benefit of humanity.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1693453.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | Proceedings of the 15th ...\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3716171\",\n      \"title\": \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU | ACM Transactions on Parallel Computing\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"PREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n* 2 Tridiagonal Matrix Solvers\\n* 2.1 Thomas Algorithm\\n* 2.1.1 Data-Parallel Thomas Algorithm.\\n* 2.2 Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) Algorithm\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nThe fast evaluation of tridiagonal solutions is fundamental to calculate spectral expansions with the polynomial connection algorithm [ 14 ] and is\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\ncyclic reduction ( PCR ) algorithm [ 8 ], Parallel Thomas algorithm ( PTA ) [ 24 ], and describe an efficient implementation combination of them for\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nThere exist multiple software packages that provide tridiagonal solvers, including proprietary Nvidia cuSPARSE library [ 16 ] for Nvidia GPUs and open\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\nIn this article, we will present PfSolve —a fast, open-source, and cross-platform implementation of a tridiagonal solver based on the runtime\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Permutation Predictions for Non-Clairvoyant Scheduling Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE Communication Lower Bounds and Optimal Algorithms for Symmetric Matrix Computations Next\\n2.2 Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) Algorithm\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/icpp/2011/4510a444/12OmNzzfTle\",\n      \"title\": \"A Scalable Tridiagonal Solver for GPUs\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2011-09-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We present the design and evaluation of a scalable tridiagonal solver targeted for GPU architectures.\\nWe observed that two distinct steps are required to solve a large tridiagonal system in parallel: 1) breaking down a problem into multiple sub problems each of which is independent of other, and 2) solving the sub problems using an efficient algorithm.\\nWe propose a hybrid method of tiled parallel cyclic reduction(tiled PCR) and thread-level parallel Thomas algorithm(p-Thomas). Algorithm transition from tiled PCR to p-Thomas is determined by input system size and hardware capability in order to achieve optimal performance.\\nThe proposed method is scalable as it can cope with various input system sizes by properly adjusting algorithmtrasition point. Our method on a NVidia GTX480 shows up to 8.3x and 49x speedups over multithreaded and sequential MKL implementations on a 3.33GHz Intel i7 975 in double precision, respectively.\\nLike what you’re reading?\\nAlready a member?\\n* Efficient Tridiagonal Solvers on Multicomputers IEEE Transactions on Computers\\n* Cyclic Reduction Tridiagonal Solvers on GPUs Applied to Mixed-Precision Multigrid IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\n* A Parallel Two-Level Hybrid Method for Tridiagonal Systems and Its Application to Fast Poisson Solvers IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\n* A Novel Compute-Efficient Tridiagonal Solver for Many-Core Architectures IEEE Transactions on Parallel & Distributed Systems\\nCopyright 2026 IEEE - All rights reserved.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/fullHtml/10.1145/3580373\",\n      \"title\": \"Tridigpu: A GPU Library for Block Tridiagonal and Banded Linear Equation Systems\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Due to the increasing amount of parallel computer architectures, parallel algorithms like the Cyclic Reduction [ 23 ], the Parallel Cyclic Reduction (PCR) [ 13 ], the Partition Method [ 39 ], and the Divide and Conquer [ 40 ] approach were later developed.\\nCyclic Reduction and PCR were implemented by many authors [ 2 , 7 , 20 , 25 , 27 ] for a GPU.\\nBatched block tridiagonal solvers [ 31 ] or batched scalar pentadiagonal solvers [ 17 , 19 ] (PCR-Penta and cuPentBatch) were implemented on GPUs, but only Kamra and Rao [ 24 ] solved large block tridiagonal systems, who developed a parallel algorithm (DPBWZA) for block tridiagonal Toeplitz-block-Toeplitz linear systems.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/1837853.1693472\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Fast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU:\\nFast tridiagonal solvers on the GPU | ACM SIGPLAN Notices\\nWe study the performance of three parallel algorithms and their hybrid variants for solving tridiagonal linear systems on a GPU: cyclic reduction (CR), parallel cyclic reduction (PCR) and recursive doubling (RD).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3580373\",\n      \"title\": \"Tridigpu: A GPU Library for Block Tridiagonal and Banded Linear Equation Systems | ACM Transactions on Parallel Computing\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-03-29\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"PREVIOUS ARTICLE Efficient Distributed Matrix-free Multigrid Methods on Locally Refined Meshes for FEM Computations Previous\\nIn this article, we present a CUDA library with a C API for solving block cyclic tridiagonal and banded systems on one GPU.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Efficient Distributed Matrix-free Multigrid Methods on Locally Refined Meshes for FEM Computations Previous\\nAndrew Davidson, Yao Zhang, and John D. Owens. 2011. An auto-tuned method for solving large tridiagonal systems on the GPU.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Efficient Distributed Matrix-free Multigrid Methods on Locally Refined Meshes for FEM Computations Previous\\nA parallel cyclic reduction algorithm for pentadiagonal systems with application to a convection-dominated Heston PDE. SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 43, 2 (Jan. 2021), C177–C202.\\nDigital Library\\nGoogle Scholar\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Efficient Distributed Matrix-free Multigrid Methods on Locally Refined Meshes for FEM Computations Previous\\nCrossref\\nGoogle Scholar\\n[20]\\nDominik Göddeke and Robert Strzodka. 2011. Cyclic reduction tridiagonal solvers on GPUs applied to mixed-precision multigrid.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea9680f45ffe5p5KC3rHmYFnBb\"\n}"
